      "evidenceText": "John M. Belk Impact Fellowship · The Hunt Institute About See more about our commitment to quality education. Our Story Learn about our commitment to improving education. Our Team Meet the team at The Hunt Institute. Board of Directors Meet our board of directors. Governor James B. Hunt, Jr. Leadership Award Learn more about our new Leadership Award. News Check out the latest from The Institute. Join Our Team See job listings and more about working for us. Our Supporters and Partners Learn more about our invaluable supporters and partners. Financials Access our financial reports. Programs See our programs for education leaders. Early Childhood Engagement Read about our early learning supports. Education Governors See how we shape effective leaders for education. Hunt-Kean Leadership Fellows Read about our program, meet our Fellows, and more. Hunt-Lee Mayors Leadership Fellowship Read about our Mayors program, meet our Mayors, and more. Hunt State Policy Fellows Read about our HSPF program, meet our Fellows, and more. State Legislators Retreats Learn about our relationships with state legislatures. Postsecondary Leaders Programs Learn how we develop higher education champions. K-12 Literacy Explore our programs to improve literacy learning. John M. Belk Impact Fellowship Learn more about The Fellowship. Our Approach Find out how we unite experts from across the country. Initiatives See how we encompass the full education continuum. Impact Find out how we gather and empower education leaders. Our Supporters and Partners Learn more about our invaluable supporters and partners. Resources News & resources on the education continuum. Blog Read the latest. News Read the latest news about The Institute. Publications Find and download publications. Webinars Register for upcoming webinars and find series recordings. Across the Aisle Finding commonalities in bipartisan education priorities. Higher Education Attainment Snapshot Read more about a current snapshot of states' higher... National Child Care Finder Scale Learn more about our newest scale on a systematic... Contact Donate About Our Story Our Team Board of Directors Governor James B. Hunt, Jr. Leadership Award News Join Our Team Our Supporters and Partners Financials Programs Early Childhood Engagement Education Governors Hunt-Kean Leadership Fellows Hunt-Lee Mayors Leadership Fellowship Hunt State Policy Fellows State Legislators Retreats Postsecondary Leaders Programs K-12 Literacy John M. Belk Impact Fellowship Our Approach Initiatives Impact Our Supporters and Partners Resources Blog News Publications Webinars Across the Aisle Higher Education Attainment Snapshot National Child Care Finder Scale Contact Donate John M. Belk Impact Fellowship Nurturing Social Impact Leaders The John M. Belk Impact Fellowship honors the legacy of John M. Belk – a man who exemplified servant leadership and consistently sought out ways to lift up others. In 2020 the John M. Belk Endowment and The Hunt Institute came together to develop The John M. Belk Impact Fellowship . Designed with the goal of building future leaders in the area of social impact, the Fellowship places students currently enrolled in community college, undergraduate, or graduate programs in North Carolina, in 10-month long internships with one of our host site partners focused on providing equitable access to quality education. Training Students to Make an Impact The John M. Belk Impact Fellowship is a 10-month (August-May), paid program that provides hands-on experience for students currently enrolled in community college, undergraduate, or graduate programs in North Carolina. Building on the success of the John M. Belk Scholarship program, and the belief that talent is universal but opportunity is not, the Fellowship serves as a training ground for future generations of social impact leaders. To gain exposure to the inner workings of organizations playing a variety of critical roles across North Carolina’s education landscape, Fellows are placed at organizations such as nonprofits, education systems and institutions, and philanthropy. Fellows will deepen their experience across a wide array of focus areas, build relation ships with one another and with education leaders, and gain marketable skills towards future employment. Applying to the Fellowship Program We review applications on a rolling basis, but applying before the deadline is highly encouraged! Applications are now open for the 2027–2028 cohort. Submit your application by December 31, 2026 at 11:59pm EST for priority consideration review. Applications will continue to be reviewed on a rolling basis until the final deadline on March 5, 2027 at 11:59pm EST. Continue scrolling for a complete application timeline and FAQs about the program. For any questions related to the Impact Fellowship, please contact us at belkimpactfellows@hunt-institute.org Applications are now open for the 2027-2028 Cohort Apply Here December 31, 2026, at 11:59pm EST: Priority consideration review deadline. March 5, 2027, at 11:59pm EST: Final application deadline. Program Overview Eligibility Requirements FAQs Program Overview Current Host Sites UNC Charlotte Dep. of Educational Leadership Carolina Demography myFutureNC Office of the Governor NC Independent Colleges & Universities EducationNC John M. Belk Endowment LatinxEd United Way of the Greater Triangle The Hunt Institute Belk Center for Community College Leadership and Research NC Department of Public Instruction UNC Systems Office NC East Alliance Program Details Impact Fellows work 14-20 hours a week with their host site. Attend monthly virtual Fireside Chats. Bi-weekly check-ins with the Impact Fellowship coordinator and weekly check-ins with your host site supervisor. In-person orientation and networking opportunities. Impact Fellows will receive a tailored experience based on their interests and preferences. This can include research spanning the education continuum, communications and social media management, and preparing policy briefing documents, legislative reports, and other supporting materials. Eligibility Requirements Eligibility to apply: Full-time community college, undergraduate, or graduate student at an accredited NC college or university interested in the fields of education, public policy, political science, data analytics, psychology, or related fields; for the duration of the program; mid August 2027 – early May 2028. Ability to commit to a minimum of 11 hours and a maximum of 20 hours per week from mid August 2027 early May 2028 Application Requirements Resume Unofficial transcript Cover letter (1) letter of recommendation 2 short response questions (250 words each) FAQs How many Fellows will be accepted? For each cohort, we expect to select 15-17 Impact Fellows. How many hours will I be expected to complete each week at my host site placement? Students should be able to commit at least 14 hours a week, not exceeding 20 hours. What exactly will I be doing? What will my time at my placement look like? Impact Fellows work on a variety of projects, from policy briefs and research, to social media efforts and quantitative analysis. We match Impact Fellows based on the needs of the host organizations and the skills and interests of the prospective Fellow. No two experiences are alike. Here are some of the anticipated responsibilities: Researching specific topics and policy issues to inform the development of materials and activities; Analyzing legislation, reports, and other documents and creating summaries and recommendations; Gathering and tracking qualitative and quantitative data Preparing briefing documents and other supporting materials for meetings and convenings Developing and/or maintaining the social media presence of partner organizations Providing support at on-site or virtual events as needed Other duties as assigned Is this a paid internship? Yes. Impact Fellows receive a competitive hourly pay. Where will I be placed? Once the final cohort has been selected and notified, placements are determined after reviewing the needs of our host organizations and the professional aspirations of prospective Fellows. What is the relationship between The Hunt Institute and the John M. Belk Endowment? The Hunt Institute is a grantee of the John M. Belk Endowment and serves as administrator for this Fellowship. I have a question that is not addressed here, who can I contact? For any questions related to the Impact Fellowship, please contact us at belkimpactfellows@hunt-institute.org .   John M. Belk Impact Fellows 2026–2027 2025–2026 2024-2025 2023-2024 2022-2023 2021-2022 2020-2021 Alexis Ashley | Duke University Sydney Barnes | University of North Carolina-Chapel Hill Citlalli Braulio-Madrid | North Carolina State University Elissa Campos | North Carolina State University Allegra DaPuzzo | UNC Chapel Hill Adrianna Fields | University of North Carolina at Greensboro Trypetus Gibson | NC Central University Nyasia Lloyd | North Carolina State University Bria Maddox | Duke University Aarushi Mandal | University of North Carolina at Charlotte Rachel Rackley | Duke University Kathryn Shumaker | North Carolina State University Raven Sizemore | University of North Carolina at Greensboro Eve Surratt | Duke University Daniel Walker | East Carolina University Derrian Wilson | UNC Chapel Hill Eden Aulis Campos | UNC Chapel Hill Alicia Davenport | Duke University Jyanne Guide | North Carolina A&T Ai’Nyiah Harriel | Meredith College Ella He | Duke University Selena Kongmany | North Carolina State University Sarah Lewisohn | UNC Chapel Hill Kassandra Ciriza Monreal | UNC Chapel Hill Bunmi Omisore | Duke University Kelly Palacios | Duke University Brigit Pierce | UNC Chapel Hill Liliana Schone | Davidson College Oscar R. Miranda Tapia | North Carolina State University Zachary Tucker | UNC Chapel Hill Arely Caba | Duke University Miles Charles | Davidson College Olivia Hayward | Duke University H