      "finalUrl": "https://dataphyte.org/career/call-for-applications-dataphyte-foundation-infra-watch-media-fellowship",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Call for Applications: Dataphyte Foundation Infra-Watch Media Fellowship | Dataphyte Foundation",
      "pageDescription": "Civic technology and data development organisation transforming how data drives decisions across Africa.",
      "evidenceText": "Call for Applications: Dataphyte Foundation Infra-Watch Media Fellowship | Dataphyte Foundation Dataphyte Foundation, in partnership with the Center for International Private Enterprise (CIPE), invites applications from Nigerian journalists to join a two-month investigative journalism fellowship on foreign-funded infrastructure reporting. Applications open on Thursday 19 November 2026 and close on Friday 27 November 2026. About the Fellowship The Foreign-Funded Infrastructure Accountability (“Infra-Watch”) Media Fellowship will train five Journalists to investigate and report on how foreign-financed infrastructure projects in Nigeria are funded via Official Development Assistance (ODA), Private Public Partnerships (PPPs), bilateral and multilateral debt financing, how the infrastructure contracts are managed, and the projects implemented, and compared to global best practices. Nigeria owes more than $46 billion in external debt , yet loan terms, procurement arrangements and project outcomes are often only partly disclosed. The fellowship equips journalists to close that gap with data, so that the public can scrutinise how capital inflows are used and identify practices that weaken transparency and institutional integrity, known as corrosive capital. This fellowship is part of Dataphyte’s Mobilising Data for Infrastructure Governance and Corrosive Capital Monitoring in Nigeria project. Fellows will work with data from Dataphyte’s Anfani portal and CIPE’s Infrastructure Watch platform for their investigation. What Fellows Will Receive Training on foreign development financing structures, loan contracts, procurement and corrosive capital risks. Hands-on training in using Anfani Beneficial Ownership and CIPE Infrastructure Watch data for investigations. Mentorship support from Dataphyte’s editorial team.. Funding to produce one investigative report on a foreign-funded infrastructure project. Publication and promotion of fellows’ stories through Dataphyte’s platforms. Eligibility The fellowship is open to Nigerian Journalists who meet all of the following criteria: Work as an accountability, investigative, business or economy Journalist as staff or freelancer. Have a track record of published or broadcast stories, with an interest in public finance, infrastructure, debt or procurement reporting. Can commit to full participation in all training sessions, mentorship and reporting milestones throughout the fellowship. Have their employer’s consent to take part (staff journalists only). Women Journalists and applicants outside Lagos and Abuja are strongly encouraged to apply. How to Apply Interested applicants should complete the application form using the link below and upload the required supporting documents. A sample of published report or multiformat production, on external aid and debt financing, especially on infrastructure projects. A story pitch on one foreign-funded infrastructure project you wish to investigate. ( Pitch Template ) A letter of consent from your employer (staff Journalists only). APPLY HERE Only shortlisted applicants will be contacted. Key Dates Call opens Thursday 19 November 2026 Application deadline Friday 27 November 2026 Shortlisted applicants contacted Friday 5 December 2026 Fellowship duration Three months. Start date to be announced to shortlisted applicants . Citation content_copy Copy citation Dataphyte Foundation (2026). “Call for Applications: Dataphyte Foundation Infra-Watch Media Fellowship.” Dataphyte Foundation. Available at: /career/call-for-applications-dataphyte-foundation-infra-watch-media-fellowship Table of contents Keep reading More from Dataphyte View all articles → Accountability Strengthening Grassroots Journalists for Data-driven Accountability Reporting: Dataphyte’s Fiscal Accountability Reporting Training in Bayelsa On November 4 and 5, 2026, Dataphyte Foundation held a two-day Fiscal Accountability Reporting Training for journalists and civic… Nov 20, 2026 Security Dataphyte Foundation, CPECD Launch Report on Security Overreach and Corruption at Checkpoints Dataphyte Foundation and the Center for Peace Education and Community Development (CPECD) have launched a report documenting… Oct 31, 2026 Agriculture Dataphyte Foundation Strengthens Women Farmers' Capacity for Evidence-Based Policy Advocacy Dataphyte Foundation has strengthened the capacity of the Small Scale Women Farmers Organisation in Nigeria (SWOFON) Secretariat… Oct 30, 2026 Behind the Bytes Check your inbox Done",
