      "id": "32ba2f12e615eae12a514fa6b0f418a2ecf27372bb20539900535dada9b1c72c",
      "title": "Tor Community Fellowship 2027 (stipend available)",
      "url": "https://opportunitydesk.org/2026/11/23/tor-community-fellowship-2027",
      "category": "opportunities",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-27T23:44:07.796Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://forum.torproject.org/t/call-for-applications-tor-community-fellowship/22490",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=utf-8",
      "pageTitle": "Call for Applications: Tor Community Fellowship",
      "pageDescription": "Hello, \nThe Tor Project is happy to announce the launch of the Tor Community Fellowship. For its first year, we are accepting 4 human rights defenders, journalists, technologists, and researchers. \nFellows will get the c…",
      "evidenceText": "Call for Applications: Tor Community Fellowship - General Discussion - Tor Project Forum = 40rem)\" rel=\"stylesheet\" data-target=\"discourse-reactions_desktop\" /> = 40rem)\" rel=\"stylesheet\" data-target=\"poll_desktop\" /> Call for Applications: Tor Community Fellowship General Discussion training , education zaatar November 17, 2026, 7:15am 1 Hello, The Tor Project is happy to announce the launch of the Tor Community Fellowship . For its first year, we are accepting 4 human rights defenders, journalists, technologists, and researchers. Fellows will get the chance to learn from the Tor Project team, build a deep understanding of how internet censorship works, how tools like Tor work to support circumvention at the network level, and develop skills in technical investigations in relation to Tor. Fellows will conclude with a small project on a related subject of their choice. This is a part-time fellowship which will span 6 months , from February to July 2027 . Fellows will be offered a stipend of 500 USD per month for the duration of the fellowship. Here’s the link to the call for applications: Nextcloud . We plan to close the call on December 17, 2026 . Eligibility Requirements Participants in the Tor Community Fellowship will be expected to: Dedicate approximately 10 to 12 hours per month to the fellowship, from February to July 2027, to participate in remote training sessions and conduct independent work outside of scheduled sessions to deepen understanding of the topics. Engage with the program cohort and the Tor Project during the remote trainings, and in discussion groups on an ad-hoc basis. Be a human rights defender, journalist, technologist, or researcher, and be from or based in one of the countries listed below. Demonstrate interest in developing a small project in a digital rights or security related topic such as internet censorship, censorship circumvention, anonymity, privacy, etc. Demonstrate past engagement in digital rights and security work, be it advocacy, investigations, training, or others. Demonstrate a good understanding of the landscape of digital threats, including censorship and surveillance faced by journalists, human rights defenders, and civil society organizations in their local contexts. Show familiarity with Tor powered apps like Tor Browser, Tails, Orbot, and others. Have an understanding of how the internet works, what IP addresses and the domain name system (DNS) are. Engage in the English language as the primary language throughout the fellowship. Have access to a computer and stable reliable internet for videoconferencing. Eligible countries: This round is open only for applicants from the following countries: Africa: Democratic Republic of the Congo, Ethiopia, Kenya, Liberia, Mozambique, Rwanda, Somalia, Sudan, Tanzania, Uganda, Zambia, Zimbabwe. Asia: Bangladesh. Europe: Russia*, Ukraine. Latin America: Bolivia, Colombia, Cuba*, Guatemala. Middle East and North Africa: Palestine, Syria. * Unfortunately, due to U.S. sanctions, The Tor Project, Inc. (a US nonprofit) is unable to directly transfer funds to individuals based in Cuba or Russia. If you have any questions, do not hesitate to reach out to me at: zaatar@torproject.org Looking forward to receiving and reading your applications, Za’atar Frequently Asked Questions Updated: 24 November 2026 If I live in country x, but I’m originally from country y, and country y is among the list of eligible countries, can I still apply? Yes, we consider where people are from rather than where they’re currently based. Is the country restriction strict? Unfortunately, yes. We can only consider applicants who are from one of the countries listed as “eligible.” I don’t have any professional reference, can I still apply? Yes, that is fine. We take into consideration references from colleagues within volunteering or community engagement activities, university professors, etc. I’m not from one of the eligible countries but I’m very interested in taking part in an online learning program and learn more about Tor. This is great and exactly what this program intends to cater for! We have many other avenues through which folks can participate and learn about Tor: you can join our channels on Matrix , participate in this forum, navigate through our learning resources , our support portal , and so much more. After all, Tor is a free and open source project with a large community of volunteers without whom Tor would not be possible. kpk November 19, 2026, 10:35am 2 Will future rounds be open to other countries like India? zaatar November 19, 2026, 12:00pm 3 That’s definitely our intention! kpk November 19, 2026, 1:33pm 4 Looking forward! ml_1136 November 25, 2026, 4:56pm 6 I’m not from Colombia, but I live here legally, although I’m still waiting for my work permit. Can I apply? LingDeeYoh November 26, 2026, 5:00pm 7 I love this so much. I am hoping future eligibility will include South Africa, too. Amazing fellowship. zaatar November 27, 2026, 6:57am 8 Thank you for your questions! So unfortunately, we’re considering where people are from rather than where they’re based in. This limitation is totally out of our control and we truly wish it wasn’t the case. But it’s absolutely our goal for next rounds to be open to all.",
