      "pageDescription": "Ad Spending Passes $7 Billion for the Cycle (MIDDLETOWN, CT) October 29, 2026 – Ad spending in U.S. Senate races continues to rise in some states but is falling in others, according to a new analysis from the Wesleyan Media Project. Table 1 shows the volume of ad spending in each of the top Senate",
      "evidenceText": "Ad Spending Up Sharply in Senate Races in Kansas, Iowa, Michigan, Nebraska and South Carolina; Spending Slows in North Carolina and Georgia - Wesleyan Media Project Skip to main content Hit enter to search or ESC to close Close Search 2026 Elections Releases Ad Spending Up Sharply in Senate Races in Kansas, Iowa, Michigan, Nebraska and South Carolina; Spending Slows in North Carolina and Georgia By Wesleyan Media Project October 29, 2026 No Comments Ad Spending Passes $7 Billion for the Cycle (MIDDLETOWN, CT) October 29, 2026 – Ad spending in U.S. Senate races continues to rise in some states but is falling in others, according to a new analysis from the Wesleyan Media Project. Table 1 shows the volume of ad spending in each of the top Senate races in both early October (September 28 to October 11) and late October (October 12 to 25). Overall spending in most states increased in the recent two-week period but did decline in Texas, Georgia and North Carolina. The latter two are states in which pro-Republican spending has dropped considerably. Indeed, 93 percent of ad spending in Georgia in the most recent two weeks was pro-Democratic, and 84 percent of spending in North Carolina was pro-Democratic. At the same time, there has been an influx of spending in Michigan, Iowa, Kansas, Nebraska and South Carolina. Ad spending in Iowa, for instance, has increased from $42 million earlier in the month to $65 million in the most recent two weeks. The percentage rise in spending in Kansas has been even steeper, going from $12 million earlier in the month to $34 million in the most recent two weeks. About 58 percent of that spending favors the Democrat, Adam Hamilton. “Following the money suggests that the Senate battleground has been changing over these past two weeks, moving away from bluer states like Georgia and North Carolina and into redder territory,” said Travis Ridout, co-director of the Wesleyan Media Project. Interestingly, the pro-Democratic share of spending has also been rising in some second-tier Senate races, such as Mississippi and Florida, though the pro-Democratic share of ad spending slipped by 20 percentage points in South Carolina, moving from 70 percent to 50 percent. Table 1: Change in Spending in Senate Races (Early October to Late October) Spending (early October) pro-Dem % (early October) Spending (late October) pro-Dem % (late October) Change in spending Change in pro-Dem % OH $100.17 38.0% $111.30 41.3% $11.13 3.3% TX $96.27 27.0% $89.15 33.2% -$7.12 6.2% MI $49.56 49.0% $65.93 48.6% $16.37 -0.4% IA $41.82 56.7% $64.59 58.7% $22.77 2.0% ME $34.24 46.1% $40.23 48.9% $5.99 2.8% KS $12.45 56.2% $34.29 57.6% $21.84 1.4% AK $22.52 47.0% $25.38 52.3% $2.86 5.3% NE $10.62 0.5%** $22.94 5.9%** $12.32 5.4% NH $21.70 41.0% $22.34 44.7% $0.64 3.7% SC $6.87 70.2% $20.14 50.1% $13.27 -20.1% GA $24.53 72.6% $17.45 93.0% -$7.08 20.4% FL $8.78 4.3% $11.68 31.7% $2.90 27.4% NC $16.54 63.7% $11.43 84.2% -$5.11 20.5% MS $1.68 39.2% $6.13 62.6% $4.45 23.4% AR $3.06 10.7% $3.16 12.4% $0.10 1.7% MN $1.71 44.0% $2.82 41.3% $1.11 -2.7% Totals include broadcast, local cable, connected TV (CTV), digital (including Meta, Google, Snapchat and X), network cable, radio, satellite, and network broadcast. **This percentage is the Democratic percentage of total. The total includes spending on behalf of the Independent candidate. CITE SOURCE OF DATA AS: AdImpact with analysis by the Wesleyan Media Project. Table 2 provides more details on spending over the past two weeks in all Senate races. For example, in Ohio in just the previous two weeks, ad sponsors have spent over $111 million. That amounts to about $8 million each day, with the majority of spending (59 percent) benefiting the GOP candidate. Over 83 percent of spending on those ads came from outside groups. This included $21 million from the pro-Democratic group WinSenate, $25 million from the pro-GOP SLF PAC, and $17 million from the GOP-aligned Defend American Jobs. In Texas, ad spending topped $89 million in the previous two weeks, with an even greater share of spending on behalf of the Republican candidate (67 percent) than in Ohio. This included $40 million from Texas PAC, which is a Super PAC reportedly backed with funds from Elon Musk. That group allocated over $2.7 million of its ad spending to spots that aired nationally instead of on local broadcast stations purchased at the media market level. This is rare but sometimes more cost efficient for outside groups, who do not qualify for the lowest unit rate (and in Texas, there are 20 media markets). Ad spending was also robust in Michigan and Iowa, which saw ad spending top $60 million in each race over the previous two weeks. Spending in Maine rounds out the top 5 at $40 million. Table 2: Ad Spending in Races for U.S. Senate (10/12/26 to 10/25/26) Race Total Spending* (in $Ms) Democratic Cand Spend (in $Ms) Republican Cand Spend (in $Ms) Outside Grp Spend (in $Ms) Group % pro-Dem % OH $111.30 $7.64 $5.30 $93.15 83.7 41.3% TX $89.15 $18.88 $3.41 $59.06 66.2 33.2% MI $65.93 $5.89 $4.02 $49.77 75.5 48.6% IA $64.59 $4.99 $3.85 $48.55 75.2 58.7% ME $40.23 $4.19 $2.11 $32.01 79.6 48.9% KS $34.29 $6.15 $2.15 $24.91 72.7 57.6% AK $25.38 $2.76 $1.61 $18.44 72.6 52.3% NE $22.94 $1.19 $19.95 87 5.9%** NH $22.34 $3.16 $1.47 $16.95 75.9 44.7% SC $20.14 $4.54 $0.01 $15.09 74.9 50.1% GA $17.45 $14.97 $0.06 $2.42 13.9 93.0% FL $11.68 $0.64 $1.68 $7.02 60.1 31.7% NC $11.43 $8.34 $0.10 $2.58 22.5 84.2% MS $6.13 $0.69 $1.61 $3.82 62.4 62.6% AR $3.16 $0.39 $1.02 $1.33 42.3 12.4% MN $2.82 $1.16 $1.13 $0.52 18.6 41.3% VA $2.77 $2.69 $0.08 2.9 100.0% MT $2.20 $0.43 $1.27 57.7 ~0%** SD $2.17 $0.61 $1.40 64.5 25.7** KY $2.13 $0.01 $1.36 $0.76 35.7 1.2% LA $1.67 $0.65 $1.01 $0.00 0.1 39.1% WV $1.41 $0.00 $1.28 $0.13 8.9 0.3% CO $1.00 $0.68 0 67.2** NM $0.90 $0.90 $0.00 0 100.0% IL $0.73 $0.58 $0.14 $0.00 0.2 80.4% ID $0.62 $0.62 0 0.0% RI $0.57 $0.57 0 100.0% WY $0.17 $0.17 $0.00 0 0.0% OK $0.16 $0.16 $0.00 0.3 0.0% DE $0.11 $0.11 0 100.0% TN $0.09 $0.05 $0.04 48.6 23.3% MA $0.08 $0.00 $0.05 $0.00 0.8 1.8%** AL $0.01 $0.00 $0.01 95.8 66.8% NJ $0.01 $0.01 0 100.0% OR $0.00 $0.00 0 100.0% Totals include broadcast, local cable, connected TV (CTV), digital (including Meta, Google, Snapchat and X), network cable, radio, satellite, and network broadcast. *Total spending will be greater in some rows than the sum of candidate and group spending because the total also includes any spending on party coordinated ads with candidates. **This percentage is the Democratic percentage of total. The total includes spending on behalf of the Independent candidate. CITE SOURCE OF DATA AS: AdImpact with analysis by the Wesleyan Media Project. OH, TX, and WI Top List of Expensive Gov Races; GOP Leads Spending in Top States Ad spending in races for governor topped $30 million in three states (Ohio, Texas, and Wisconsin) over the last two weeks and was just shy of that amount in two more (Georgia and Florida). Spending exceeded $10 million in nine states. Republicans continue to have considerable ad spending advantages in several states, including Ohio, Texas, Wisconsin, Georgia, Florida and Nevada, though Democrats have spending advantages in New York, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Iowa. In Ohio, a lot of attention was focused on one ad aired by the Republican candidate, Vivek Ramaswamy. In that ad, the mother of Ramaswamy’s opponent, Amy Acton, appeared and urged voters to vote for the Republican candidate, arguing that her daughter was a liar. The ad was widely criticized because Acton and her mother were long estranged over a sexual assault allegation against Acton’s stepfather, who was charged with raping Acton when she was 12 years old. Acton and her brother were subsequently removed from their mother’s custody, and Acton has said she has had no contact with her mother since. The ad aired in the timeline covered for this report, largely between 10/22 and 10/25. The spot aired about 700 times, costing Ramaswamy around $650,000. “In every election cycle a particular ad often becomes the focus of national discussion, either because it is innovative, particularly memorable, or very negative,” said Michael Franz, co-director of the Wesleyan Media Project. “The ad from Ramaswamy featuring Amy Acton’s mother may very well be the one ad people remember most about the ad wars in 2026.” In Ohio and Texas, the combination of competitive Senate and gubernatorial campaigns means that in just the last two weeks, voters in Ohio have seen over $150 million in ads across the two races, and in Texas the total across the Senate and governor races is about $130 million. Table 3: Ad Spending in Races for Governor (10/12/26 to 10/25/26) Race Total Spending* (in $Ms) Democratic Cand Spend (in $Ms) Republican Cand Spend (in $Ms) Outside Grp Spend (in $Ms) Group % pro-Dem % OH $43.56 $8.49 $9.95 $25.12 57.7 31.3% TX $39.14 $8.55 $28.71 $1.74 4.4 21.8% WI $33.03 $8.30 $8.44 $16.29 49.3 43.9% GA $29.72 $0.04 $22.02 $0.28 1 25.8% FL $27.67 $1.13 $2.25 $1.14 4.1 19.8% NY $17.06 $8.01 $4.47 $4.59 26.9 70.5% NV $16.63 $1.05 $1.19 $14.38 86.5 36.9% MI $12.90 $2.48 $2.27 $6.48 50.2 65.5% PA $10.34 $8.11 $2.23 $0.01 0.1 78.5% IA $8.44 $4.69 $3.42 $0.32 3.8 58.9% AZ $8.34 $0.50 $0.28 $4.23 50.7 43.2% KS $7.69 $0.56 $0.56 $6.57 85.5 30.6% TN $7.62 $0.25 $1.38 $3.92 51.5 3.3%** OR $6.44 $3.34 $3.10 $0.00 0 51.9% CA $5.49 $5.37 $0.10 $0.02 0.3 98.2% MN $5.33 $1.60 $0.53 $1.83 34.4 46.2% CT $4.36 $1.73 $2.64 0 39.6% IL $4.27 $4.27 $0.00 0 99.9% NE $3.98 $1.42 $2.56 $0.01 0.1 35.7% OK $3.46 $0.42 $0.61 $2.43 70.2 12.1% AL $3.34 $0.68 $2.55 $0.11 3.3 20.8% MA $3.14 $2.14 $0.89 $0.11 3.5 72.1% NM $2.67 $1.71 $0.17 $0.79 29.7 91.4% MD $1.79 $1.76 $0.03 $0.00 0 98.1% ME $1.47 $0.79 $0.06 $0.61 41.8 89.7% AR $1.46 $1.46 0 0.0% NH $1.40 $0.24 $1.16 0 17.2% CO $1.30 $0.40 $0.90 69.4 100.0% AK $1.19 $0.69 $0.18 $0.31 26.3 81.9% RI $1.07 $0.51 $0.00 $0.49 46 94.4** VT $0.88 $0.18 $0.1