      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://variety.com/2026/politics/columns/haley-stevens-anderson-cooper-interview-column-1236937200/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Variety",
      "pageDescription": "Congresswoman Haley Stevens&#039; CNN interview with Anderson Cooper about her Mike Rogers endorsement proves she shouldn&#039;t have won the Michigan primary",
      "evidenceText": "Haley Stevens Anderson Cooper Interview Is Unsettling Haley Stevens Anderson Cooper Interview Is Unsettling &times; Nov 1, 2026 9:31pm PT Haley Stevens’ Unsettling CNN Interview Proves Why She’s Unfit for Public Office By Alison Herman Plus Icon Alison Herman TV Critic aherman2006 Latest ‘Blade Runner 2099’ Review: Hunter Schafer Cements Her Status as a Genre Star, With Help from a Stunning Backdrop 4 days ago ‘The Altruists’ Review: Netflix FTX Series Fails to Get a Handle on Crypto Scammer Sam Bankman-Fried 1 week ago ‘The Gilded Age’ Star Denée Benton on Peggy’s Big Day: ‘It Was Like Eating Candy and Steak All at the Same Time’ 2 weeks ago See All Add us as a Google Preferred Source Courtesy of CNN Comments Watching Congresswoman Haley Stevens attempt to explain her 11th-hour endorsement of Republican Mike Rogers over her fellow Democrat Abdul El-Sayed in Tuesday’s election to become the next United States Senator from Michigan, my primary feeling was one of relief. Thank God this person didn’t beat El-Sayed in the primary, despite outspending him in a landslide. Thank God this person isn’t eligible for higher office, now putting her unfitness to serve on full display. Thank God this person won’t be on our television screens much longer. Related Stories Grammy Nominations 2027: Raye Leads With 11 Noms as Rosalía, Olivia Rodrigo, Ella Langley and Other Female Artists Dominate Rosalía, Hilary Duff, and Slayyyter to Be Honored at Variety’s Hitmakers Event Stevens appeared on CNN Sunday night for an excruciating 15-minute conversation with Anderson Cooper , who remained calm and quietly incredulous in his line of questioning. Cooper zeroed in on an allegation by Stevens’ fellow Congresswoman Debbie Dingell, the elder stateswoman of the Michigan delegation, that Stevens had assured Dingell a cross-party endorsement was not forthcoming mere hours before appearing alongside Rogers at a Detroit church. What changed between that phone call and the next morning? What was the deciding factor in Stevens’ about-face? here's the entirety of the seeming mental breakdown Haley Stevens just had on CNN. One of the most bizarre and incoherent political interviews I can recall. Note how she's completely unable to explain why she changed her mind from supporting Abdul El-Sayed last night to endorsing… pic.twitter.com/0QVBX6yOXf — Aaron Rupar (@atrupar) November 2, 2026 Stevens proved unable to answer these simple, direct questions. (Dingell herself called the segment “one of the most painful interviews I have ever watched” mere minutes later on the very same program.) Stevens’ demeanor, all-important in television appearances since Richard Nixon’s famous debate meltdown in 1960, was the opposite of assured. Reflexively beginning each answer with “Look…” before failing to make a coherent point, Stevens blinked, scowled and furrowed her brow through a series of evasive non-answers. One of her favored talking points was a non-specific complaint about El-Sayed’s supposed anger, but it wasn’t El-Sayed whose evident ire toward their primary opponent was on full display. Cooper largely allowed Stevens the rope with which to enthusiastically end her own political career. As Stevens repeatedly insisted how much she loves the Democratic Party, Cooper refrained from pointing out that her public refusal to vote for a Democrat was the whole premise of their conversation. As Stevens brought up the former Maine senatorial candidate Graham Platner’s Nazi tattoo and sexual assault allegations, Cooper didn’t ask what alleged actions of El-Sayed’s rose anywhere near that level. He simply bore down on the narrow, yet apparently impossible question at hand: why were Stevens’ issues with El-Sayed — like campaigning with Hasan Piker, or an ad linking Israel to high prices produced by an outside group unaffiliated with the campaign — surmountable in one moment, yet worth a public defection at a critical moment for a high-stakes midterm the next? Stevens also sought to downplay the significance of her decision (“I’m an independent woman”), an odd posture for a woman seizing on the attention it’s won her with a national TV appearance to take. This irony Cooper could not resist calling out, since it’s too much to take. Stevens could have confined her support of Rogers to the privacy of the voting booth, a shameful yet private concession to personal animus over political principle. Instead, Stevens chose to self-immolate, then double down on her own worst instincts with an exchange that clarified nothing except the eminent wisdom of Michigan Democrats in refusing to make her their party’s chosen standard-bearer. Then again, you can’t quite blame Stevens for basking in a highly concentrated and rapidly fading spotlight. Many of her staff has quit, as was evident in her lack of preparation for Cooper’s easily predictable queries; as Stevens herself admitted to Cooper, she’s a lame duck who won’t be serving in the House in just a few months. By throwing in her lot with Rogers and earning the public praise of Donald Trump, Stevens has guaranteed both her temporary relevance and the fact that said relevance will be vanishingly brief. The stammering, evasive person on CNN clearly wasn’t ready for primetime, ironically demonstrated in actual primetime. As disgraceful as her conduct may be, the American public can breathe a sigh of relief that we’ve dodged at least one bullet. Jump to Comments Candace Cameron Bure Says She Still Gets ‘Substantial’ ‘Full House’ Residuals and Wasn’t Surprised the Olsen Twins Skipped ‘Fuller House’: No One ‘Stayed Close’ With Them ‘Full House’ Star Jodie Sweetin Says She Had ‘$190 in My Bank Account’ Before Filming ‘Fuller House’: ‘I Had to Borrow Money the First Week’ Loading comments... Most Popular ‘Ramayana’ Doubled Visitors at Kerala’s Jatayu Rock, Official Says as India Pitches Itself to Global Productions at WAVES Film… ‘The Pitt’ Sets Season 3 Release Date at HBO Max ‘High Fidelity’ Director Stephen Frears Still Teases Jack Black: ‘I Told Him, You Owe All This to Me!’ ‘The Hawk’ Starring Will Ferrell Canceled After One Season at Netflix Ariana Grande Says ‘Petal’ Music Video Backlash Felt Like ‘Black Mirror’ and ‘I Wouldn't Change a Thing. I’d Put It Out Again Today As Is… Luca Guadagnino Apologizes for Controversial Costume Design Comments but Defends Working With Fashion Designers: They're More ‘in Touch With the… Candace Cameron Bure Says She Still Gets 'Substantial' 'Full House' Residuals and Wasn't Surprised the Olsen Twins Skipped 'Fuller House': No One… Justin Timberlake Was ‘Really Reluctant’ to Make Tell-All Documentary: ‘I’ve Gone Through a Lot in the Last Five Years’ Danny Boyle Backs Jack O'Connell as a Bond Villain, but Says He 'Would Have Been Great' as 007 Richard Hammond Says He Was Approached About ‘Top Gear’ Return, but Decided: ‘No, I’m Too Old’ Must Read Film The 100 Greatest Movie Stars of All Time By Andrew Wallenstein 1 week Film Skydance Succession: As David Ellison Sets His Team, Heads Are Rolling as Hollywood Finds Out Who’s Really in Charge Film Sex, Love and War: How the Racy Gay Epic 'La Bola Negra' Could Win Netflix's First Best Picture Oscar Film Ben Affleck Explains It All: Artists Equity, the Death of Movie Stars and the Dilemma With Comic Book Movies Film Pete Davidson Grows Up: On Leaving 'SNL,' Famous Exes, the Joys of Fatherhood and Why He's Keeping His Hillary Clinton Tattoo",
